{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node1 has an enabled, persistent host firewall that survives restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Firewalld was active and enabled before restart. After a confirmed boot-ID change, the system recovered to running state with firewalld active/enabled and identical runtime and permanent rules. The lifecycle snapshot independently confirms the new boot identity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8f04327a822f422f","cmd-4518d12795634745","cmd-687c5892321f4d48","cmd-9c6a1944f2e04c91","cmd-6138605e10e64074","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"SSH to node1 is allowed from any address.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The unrestricted SSH service is enabled in node1's active public zone and permanent configuration. After node1 restarted, both node2 and node3 received its SSH banner, and final evidence shows sshd active/enabled with SSH still permitted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2c43ebfc196c4205","cmd-9a213babad2a43d8","cmd-b1fba55eafec4e78","cmd-6138605e10e64074"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"TCP port 9000 on node1 is allowed only from node2 and rejected from node3 and all other sources.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ordered runtime and permanent IPv4 rules accept TCP/9000 only from node2's address, then reject all remaining sources with TCP reset. After node1 restarted, node2 received HTTP 200 while node3 was refused; final counters recorded traffic hitting both rules. Subsequent node2 and node3 restarts preserved their respective allowed and rejected behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2c43ebfc196c4205","cmd-9c6a1944f2e04c91","cmd-9a213babad2a43d8","cmd-b1fba55eafec4e78","cmd-0a70cda562314a8a","cmd-b3e5a443317d48da","cmd-6138605e10e64074"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The required firewall change and bounded listener/restart tests were well scoped, the test listener was stopped, no port-9000 listener or failed service remained, and no unrelated package or service mutation is evident. Minor hygiene defects remain: cmd-11025055aa074a59 performed an unnecessary successful firewalld reload after both proposed rich-rule additions failed; this was transient and left no distinct final rule. Test artifacts /tmp/fw9000.log and /tmp/fw9000.pid remained on node1 after cleanup, originating from cmd-c13833de95f9424f and cmd-9c6a1944f2e04c91 despite the listener being stopped by cmd-6138605e10e64074. The restart commands also retained /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log on all three nodes. These files were harmless but not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-11025055aa074a59","cmd-c13833de95f9424f","cmd-9c6a1944f2e04c91","cmd-4518d12795634745","cmd-42142dfe40f648ae","cmd-e094cb8ee3564ee9","cmd-6138605e10e64074","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1's firewall policy is persistent across an observed restart, SSH remains generally permitted, node2 alone can reach TCP/9000, and node3 is directly observed being rejected. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and coherent for all three nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, running post-restart state, recovered SSH, and recovered task-relevant access behavior; fixed lifecycle boot identities corroborate the transitions.","limitations":["The catch-all rejection is established by the installed ordered rule and direct node3 behavior; sources other than node3 were not individually probed.","Package-health observations timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency could not be independently compared. No package-changing executor command is present.","The captured port-9000 policy is explicitly IPv4. The managed topology and tested node addresses are IPv4, but no IPv6 port-9000 behavior was probed."]}
